##:
net/udp -- UdpSocket, the datagram half.

The module ships as bundled .sushi source and is merged as a compilation unit
when imported (`use <net/udp>`). It composes the <net/socket> primitives; the
struct is a typed name for a descriptor and carries nothing else.

A datagram socket has no peer of its own, so every send names its destination
and every receive answers with a Datagram: the bytes, and who sent them. That
is not an ergonomic choice -- an unconnected socket cannot be asked afterwards,
because the sender exists only at the instant its datagram arrives.

The close rule is <net/tcp>'s: one binding owns a socket, s.close()
ends it and writes -1 back, and a copy taken before the close holds a
descriptor the kernel may have given to somebody else.

Limitations: blocking sockets only. A datagram longer than the maximum asked
for is truncated and the rest is lost, which is how the protocol works. Every
send resolves its destination, so a tight loop to one peer resolves it each
time round.
:##

use <net/socket>
use <net/error>

##: One bound UDP socket. :##
public struct UdpSocket:
    ##: The descriptor, or -1 once close() has run. :##
    i32 fd

##:
Binds a datagram socket to an address and port.

An empty host means the wildcard address. Port 0 asks the kernel to choose,
and .local_port() reads back what it chose.

- Parameter host: The address to bind, or an empty string for the wildcard.
- Parameter port: The port to bind, or 0 to let the kernel choose.
- Returns: The bound socket.
- Errors: NetError, when the address cannot be bound.
:##
public fn bind(string host, i32 port) UdpSocket | NetError:
    let i32 fd = sock_udp_bind(host, port)??
    return Result.Ok(UdpSocket(fd))

##:
Sends one datagram to a named destination.

The destination is resolved on every call, so a loop that sends to one peer
resolves that peer each time round.

- Parameter data: The bytes to send. The buffer stays the caller's.
- Parameter host: The destination host, as a name or an address.
- Parameter port: The destination port.
- Returns: How many bytes were sent.
- Errors: NetError, when the destination is unknown or the send failed.
:##
extend UdpSocket send_to(u8[] data, string host, i32 port) i32 | NetError:
    return sock_udp_send_to(self.fd, data, host, port)??

##:
Waits for one datagram, and answers it with its sender.

A datagram longer than `max` is truncated and the rest is lost. A sender whose
address cannot be rendered leaves peer_ip empty rather than failing the
receive: the bytes did arrive.

- Parameter max: The most bytes to take from one datagram.
- Returns: The bytes, and the address and port they came from.
- Errors: NetError, when the receive failed or its bound expired.
:##
extend UdpSocket recv_from(i32 max) Datagram | NetError:
    return sock_udp_recv_from(self.fd, max)??

##:
The port a socket actually bound.

This is the only way to learn the port after binding port 0.

- Returns: The bound port.
- Errors: NetError, when the socket is not bound.
:##
extend UdpSocket local_port() i32 | NetError:
    return sock_local_port(self.fd)??

##:
Bounds how long a receive and a send on a socket may wait.

A bound that expires answers NetError.TimedOut.

- Parameter recv_ms: The receive bound, in milliseconds.
- Parameter send_ms: The send bound, in milliseconds.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when the option could not be set.
:##
extend UdpSocket set_timeouts(i32 recv_ms, i32 send_ms) ~ | NetError:
    sock_set_recv_timeout(self.fd, recv_ms)??
    sock_set_send_timeout(self.fd, send_ms)??
    return ~

##:
Closes a socket and writes -1 into the binding.

The binding is marked so that closing it again is a success. A COPY of the
socket taken before this call still holds the old descriptor; one binding owns
a socket.

- Returns: Nothing. The binding reads -1 afterwards.
- Errors: NetError, when the descriptor could not be closed.
:##
extend UdpSocket close(poke self) ~ | NetError:
    if (self.fd < 0):
        return ~
    let i32 fd = self.fd
    self.fd := -1
    sock_close(fd)??
    return ~
